- Bind release provenance to the exact builder, benchmark spec, and source-audit
  spec hashes; refuse to build a release candidate from a dirty worktree.
- Replace incomplete subject answer conclusions with explicit canonical results,
  and update third-party notices to list every source and its role.

2026-11-12
----------

Acceptance tooling / Engine 0.1.8

- Replace per-version owner review pages with one canonical entry at
  `reports/acceptance/index.html`. The builder discovers every local
  `data/<version>/` package, keeps historical and future versions in one
  archive, and exposes `#v=<version>&q=<item-id>` deep links plus a
  `?view=versions` archive route.
- Preserve the established three-column review flow: search, paper/type/status
  filters, agreement/correction/dispute decisions, keyboard navigation, local
  progress, CSV export, and owner sign-off JSON generation. Signed and archived
  versions remain browsable but strictly read-only.
- Fix packet source projection so `answer_source_id`, `answer_locator`,
  `secondary_source_id`, and `secondary_locator` resolve to the correct
  publisher, page, and answer image. Historical manifests now fall back to
  `dataset_license`, and manifest-recorded owner audits are shown as completed
  even when the local review packet is absent.
- Move the dashboard templates into `gaokao_bench/acceptance_ui/`, add
  `gaokao-bench acceptance` and `scripts/build_acceptance_dashboard.py`, and
  convert the two former single-version builders into compatibility shims so a
  second acceptance template cannot drift back into use.
- Add synthetic contract tests for version discovery, candidate/signed/archive
  modes, source mapping, asset deduplication, the single output path, and the
  CLI entry point. No dataset was republished and no owner-attestation state was
  changed.

Engine 0.1.8 / Scorer 0.1.6 / Summary schema 2.4

- Centralize release-facing scoring metadata in one builder used by both the
  public manifest and `RELEASE_CARD.json`; the two files can no longer drift
  independently from the benchmark spec.
- Require every explicit scoring-policy field for `0.1+` datasets built with
  Engine 0.1.8 or newer. Immutable `0.0.x` and pre-contract
  `0.1.0-alpha.*` packages retain their historical manifest shape; the legacy
  path is never selected for a `0.1+` dataset when a modern engine version is
  recorded.
- Reject a new package that catalogues subjective items without declaring
  `scoring.catalog_task_types`. Historical `0.0.x` packages are exempt because
  their immutable specs predate the task-eligibility contract.
- Sanitize run-level coverage in compact JSON, including nested coverage copied
  into `scope`. Human reports now separate the selected run scope from full
  dataset coverage instead of presenting them as one ratio.
- Record `scoring_policy` in compact evaluation and run summaries, and add
  question-count coverage fractions to the shared coverage view.
- Replace the 2026 builder's hard-coded engine version with the package version
  source of truth.
- Make the 2026 builder refuse to overwrite a non-empty data package directory;
  rebuilt candidates must use a new immutable dataset version and output path.
- No dataset was republished and no owner-attestation state was changed by this
  engine release.

Engine 0.1.7 / Scorer 0.1.5 / Summary schema 2.3

- Make task eligibility part of the versioned scoring contract. A spec now
  declares `primary_task_types`, `catalog_task_types`, `credit_policy`, and
  `process_policy`; primary and catalog types cannot overlap, and every
  declared task type must belong to one of those two groups.
- Keep exactly one primary metric, `score.weighted_points`. Scored questions
  use only `choice` or `slots`; rubric/catalog questions stay outside the
  denominator. The current credit policy is all-or-nothing and process credit
  is excluded from the primary score. The canonical contract also rejects a
  renamed primary metric.
- Expand `coverage` from a selected-question view into closed dataset metadata:
  scored questions/points, catalog questions/points, full-paper questions/points,
  closure flags, and selected/full-paper fractions. Coverage remains report
  metadata and is not a second leaderboard score.
- Verify full-paper closure during dataset validation for both questions and
  points. Existing 2025 packages close at 74 + 26 = 100 questions and
  353 + 397 = 750 points; the 2026 National I candidate closes at
  14 + 5 = 19 questions and 73 + 77 = 150 points.
- Add `diagnostic.full_credit_rate` for new specs. Immutable `0.0.x` packages
  keep `diagnostic.exact_accuracy` as the legacy ID with the same semantics.
- Bump the 2026 National I benchmark spec to 1.1 with explicit primary/catalog
  eligibility and the new full-credit diagnostic.
- Print a compact coverage line in evaluation and run summaries while keeping
  selected question-ID arrays out of compact JSON.
- No dataset was republished and no owner-attestation state was changed by this
  engine release.

